The code statements that lead to the bug are:
return pci_seg->rlookup_table[devid];
Reason:
The function `__rlookup_amd_iommu()` accesses `pci_seg->rlookup_table[devid]` without verifying that `devid` is within the valid range (≤ `pci_seg->last_bdf`). When a PCI device’s BDF is not described by the IVRS, an out-of-bounds read occurs. Before commit e874c666b15b, the table was a zeroed page-order allocation, so the over-read returned NULL and the caller’s NULL check skipped the device. After that commit, the table is a tight kvcalloc(), and the over-read returns adjacent slab contents, which `check_device()` then dereferences as a `struct amd_iommu *`, causing a general protection fault.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (pci_seg->id != seg)
	continue;
/* IVRS may not describe every device on the bus */
if (devid > pci_seg->last_bdf)
	return NULL;
return pci_seg->rlookup_table[devid];
Reason:
The fix adds a bounds check comparing `devid` against `pci_seg->last_bdf` before indexing into `rlookup_table`. If `devid` exceeds the last valid BDF, the function returns NULL, which causes the caller (`check_device()` → `amd_iommu_probe_device()`) to skip the device cleanly. This prevents the out-of-bounds read and the subsequent general protection fault.

